{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-103962",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The series expansion of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)=x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-⋯\\,.\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $-{x}^{2}$ in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-{x}^{2}) & =(-{x}^{2})-\\frac{1}{2}(-{x}^{2})^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}(-{x}^{2})^{3}-⋯ \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{6}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, integrating with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{6}-⋯)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =C-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{10}-\\frac{{x}^{7}}{21}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
